 "claims": {
  "explanation": "Isoxsuprine is a beta-adrenergic agonist that also inhibits CDK1 (IC50 ~2.9 uM), and its selective killing likely reflects dependence on CDK1/ADRB2-linked signaling rather than a single lineage. Lines with high ADRB2 expression or intact beta-adrenergic/cAMP signaling would be sensitized, while loss of CDK1-pathway dependency or protective mutations in cell-cycle/apoptotic regulators would reduce killing. The transcriptional similarity to adrenergic and histamine/dopamine receptor antagonists supports a G-protein-coupled receptor-mediated component.",
